lgebras: a digraph G is diagonal if and only if the distance algebra σG is Koszul over any field, and if and only if G satisfies and the path cochain algebra is Koszul over any field. To provide a source of examples of digraphs, we study the extended Hasse diagram {\\stackrel{ˆ}{G}}_{K} of a pure simplicial complex K. For a triangulation K of a topological manifold M, we express the non-diagonal part of the magnitude homology of {\\stackrel{ˆ}{G}}_{K} in terms of the homology of M. As a corollary, we obtain that if K is a triangulation of a closed manifold M, then {\\stackrel{ˆ}{G}}_{K} is diagonal if and only if M is a homology sphere.\n\n## MSC\n\n05C20\n\n;\n\n16S37\n\n;\n\n55Uxx\n\n;\n\n57Q15\n\n## Keywords\n\nMagnitude homology\n\n;\n\nGraphs\n\n;\n\nKoszul algebras\n\n;\n\nHomology spheres\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacterizing positroid quotients of uniform matroids (https://alco.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/alco.475.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Since whether one positroid is a quotient of another is deter- ... A canonical “local property” of flag positroids is the “Grassmann necklace con- ... ample for [11, Conjecture 6.3], as shown in Example 5.3, also shows that Theorem 1.4\ncharacterize the quotients among lattice path matroids.\nTheorem 1.4. Let M and N be lattice path matroids. Then M is a quotient of N\nif and only if the Grassmann necklace of N contains the Grassmann necklace of M\nentrywise, and the Grassmann conecklace of N contains the Grassmann conecklace of\nM entrywise.\nTheorem 1.4 demonstrates that certain “local properties” may naturally “globalize”\nfor lattice path matroids but not for general positroids. In fact, the same counterex-\nample for [11, Conjecture 6.3], as shown in Example 5.3, also shows that Theorem 1.4\nis not true for general positroids.\nWe remark that a complete characterization of flag lattice path matroids was al-\nrea